# Myers Diff

**An implementation of the Myers diff algorithm** — the O((N+M)D) shortest edit script algorithm that finds the minimal set of insertions and deletions to transform one text into another. This is the same algorithm Git uses internally for `git diff`.

## Why It Matters

The Myers algorithm, published by Eugene Myers in 1986, is the gold standard for line-based text differencing. It finds the **shortest edit script (SES)** — the minimum number of insertions and deletions needed to transform sequence A into sequence B. This is equivalent to finding the shortest path in an edit graph.

**Why not just LCS?** The classic Longest Common Subsequence approach uses O(N×M) time and space. Myers' algorithm runs in O((N+M)×D) time where D is the edit distance — so when the texts are similar (small D), it's nearly linear. For typical code changes (a few lines different), this is dramatically faster than the full matrix approach.

**Key properties:**
- **Optimal diff** — Always finds the minimum number of changes
- **Fast for similar texts** — O(ND) where D = edit distance (small for typical code changes)
- **Linear-space variant** — Divide-and-conquer mode for large files (like Git's `--minimal`)

**Used by:** Git (`xdiff` library), Google's Diff Match Patch library, and virtually every modern version control system.

## How It Works

Myers' algorithm treats the diff problem as a **shortest path in an edit graph**. The graph has vertices at each (x, y) coordinate where x indexes the old text and y indexes the new text. Three types of edges exist:

- **Diagonal (free)** — When `old[x] == new[y]`, you can move diagonally (matching line, no edit cost)
- **Horizontal (cost 1)** — Move right = delete a line from old
- **Vertical (cost 1)** — Move down = insert a line from new

The algorithm searches for the shortest path from (0,0) to (N,M) using a BFS-like approach that explores all paths of cost D before any path of cost D+1.

**The k-diagonal trick:** Myers observes that all vertices reachable with cost D on diagonal k (where k = x − y) can be computed from the cost D−1 diagonals. This means each iteration only processes O(D) diagonals, not the full O(N×M) grid.

**Backtracking:** Once the endpoint (N,M) is reached, the algorithm backtracks through the saved search traces to reconstruct the actual sequence of edit operations (Equal, Insert, Delete).

**Unified diff output:** The `to_unified` method groups edit operations into hunks with configurable context lines, producing standard unified diff format compatible with `patch` and Git.

**Linear-space variant:** For inputs >1000 lines, the algorithm switches to a divide-and-conquer approach (Hirschberg's technique) that finds the midpoint of the optimal path and recurses on each half — reducing space to O(N+M) while maintaining the time complexity.

## Quick Start

```rust
use diff_myers::MyersDiff;

let old = "fn main() {\n    println!(\"hello\");\n}";
let new = "fn main() {\n    println!(\"hello, world\");\n    println!(\"done\");\n}";

let diff = MyersDiff::diff(old, new);

// Stats
println!("+{} -{} (distance: {})",
    diff.insertions(), diff.deletions(), diff.edit_distance());

// Unified diff format
let unified = diff.to_unified("old.rs", "new.rs", 3);
print!("{}", unified);
// Output:
// --- old.rs
// +++ new.rs
// @@ -1,3 +1,4 @@
//  fn main() {
// -    println!("hello");
// +    println!("hello, world");
// +    println!("done");
//  }
```

## API

### `MyersDiff`
- `diff(old: &str, new: &str) -> Self` — Standard Myers diff. O((N+M)D)
- `diff_linear(old: &str, new: &str) -> Self` — Linear-space divide-and-conquer variant. O((N+M)D) time, O(N+M) space
- `to_unified(old_label, new_label, context) -> String` — Render as unified diff with N context lines
- `insertions() -> usize` — Count of inserted lines
- `deletions() -> usize` — Count of deleted lines
- `edit_distance() -> usize` — Total insertions + deletions (the D in O(ND))
- `ops: Vec<EditOp>` — The raw edit operations

### `EditOp`
- `Equal(String)` — Unchanged line
- `Insert(String)` — Line added from new text
- `Delete(String)` — Line removed from old text

## Architecture Notes

This is the core differencing engine in SuperInstance's text processing toolkit, used by the version control system, collaborative editing features, and patch generation. The Patience diff and three-way merge libraries build on top of this algorithm.

